<h2>Two chickpeas, two markets</h2>
<p>"Chickpeas" as a generic order request is about as useful as ordering "rice" without specifying variety — technically an order, but not one that gets you a meaningful, comparable quote. Kabuli and desi are different products commercially, serving different end uses, and priced independently of each other.</p>

<h2>Kabuli chickpeas</h2>
<p>Larger, lighter-coloured, thin-skinned, and used whole — the type most familiar internationally as the base for hummus and similar preparations. Graded by count per ounce: a 42-44 count means roughly 42 to 44 chickpeas per ounce (larger individual chickpeas), while a 58-60 count means smaller chickpeas at more per ounce. Lower count numbers command higher prices, since large, uniform kabuli chickpeas are harder to grow consistently at scale.</p>

<h2>Desi chickpeas</h2>
<p>Smaller, darker and thicker-skinned, desi chickpeas are more frequently processed — split and husked into chana dal — for South Asian culinary use, though whole desi is also traded. Desi has a broader growing footprint across India and is generally the lower-cost type, though pricing still depends on grade and quality within the category.</p>

<h2>Specification beyond type</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Count</strong> (kabuli specifically) — your target size grade</li>
  <li><strong>Form</strong> (desi) — whole or split (chana dal)</li>
  <li><strong>Moisture</strong> — ≤12% typical</li>
  <li><strong>Admixture and defect tolerance</strong> — damaged, discoloured, split percentage</li>
  <li><strong>Purity</strong> — the percentage of the stated type in the lot</li>
</ul>

<h2>Getting this right on your first enquiry</h2>
<p>Tell us your end application — hummus and whole-use products point toward kabuli at a specific count; dal and South Asian culinary use points toward desi, whole or split. That single piece of information determines almost everything else about how we source and quote your order, so it's worth settling before requesting a price.</p>

<h2>A common mistake worth avoiding</h2>
<p>Buyers new to sourcing chickpeas sometimes request "the best quality available" without specifying type or count, expecting the supplier to identify the ideal product for an unstated use case. This rarely works well — "best" means something different for a hummus manufacturer needing large, uniform kabuli than for a dal processor needing consistent desi for splitting. Coming to the conversation with your end application defined, even loosely, gets you a far more useful first quote than an open-ended request does.</p>

<h2>Sample before you commit to a new count grade</h2>
<p>If you're moving to a new count grade you haven't sourced before — say, stepping up from 58-60 to a premium 42-44 kabuli — request a sample first. Count grading has some natural batch-to-batch variation even within a stated range, and seeing actual product before a bulk order confirms it meets your visual and size expectations, not just the number on a specification sheet. This is a small extra step that prevents a much larger disagreement after a full container has already landed at your port.</p>
